====Usage notes====
Deriving from its original senses of {{m|en|skillful}}, {{m|en|capable}}, {{m|en|deft}}, {{m|en|clever}}, and otherwise ''having things well [[in hand]]'', ''handsome'' as a synonym of {{m|en|good-looking}} was initially primarily focused on the well-proportioned and noble aspect of a person embodying those positive traits. Over the course of the 19th & 20th centuries, it developed into a more general and gendered idea of male beauty distinguished from the feminine beauty thought to be implied by words like {{m|en|pretty}}, {{m|en|lovely}}, {{m|en|beautiful}}, and so on. In reference to men, it continues to have a more aesthetic and general sense than {{m|en|hot}}, {{m|en|manly}}, {{m|en|sexy}}, and similar words primarily focused on sexual attraction. In reference to women, present use of ''handsome'' can seem old fashioned; when it does appear, depending on context, it may continue the original sense of powerful or statuesque beauty or imply a mannish or otherwise unusual form of beauty. Similarly, present use of ''handsome'' for inanimate objects can seem awkward; when it does appear, it usually implies the object is well built, cute and well proportioned.

